Let the three positive integers be $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$
We know that $a \equiv 25 \pmod{47},$ $b \equiv 20 \pmod{47},$ and $c \equiv 3 \pmod{47}$.

We want to find $(a+b+c) \pmod{47}$.
Using the properties of modular arithmetic, we can rearrange the congruences as follows:
\begin{align*}
a &\equiv 25 \pmod{47} \\
b &\equiv 20 \pmod{47} \\
c &\equiv 3 \pmod{47}
\end{align*}

Adding these congruences, we get:
\begin{align*}
a+b+c &\equiv 25 + 20 + 3 \pmod{47} \\
&\equiv 48 \pmod{47}
\end{align*}

Since $48 \equiv 1 \pmod{47}$, the remainder when $a+b+c$ is divided by $47$ is $\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1

