solution_steps:
We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor of $9n - 2$ and $7n + 3$.
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(9n - 2, 7n + 3) &= \text{gcd}(7n + 3, 9n - 2 - (7n + 3) \cdot 1) \\
&= \text{gcd}(7n + 3, 2n - 5).
\end{align*}
We can simplify this equation further:
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(7n + 3, 2n - 5) &= \text{gcd}(2n - 5, 7n + 3 - (2n - 5) \cdot 3) \\
&= \text{gcd}(2n - 5, 7n + 3 - (6n - 15)) \\
&= \text{gcd}(2n - 5, n + 18).
\end{align*}
At this point, we can use the Euclidean algorithm again:
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(2n - 5, n + 18) &= \text{gcd}(n + 18, 2n - 5 - (n + 18) \cdot 2) \\
&= \text{gcd}(n + 18, -41).
\end{align*}
The only positive integer $n$ for which $n + 18$ and $-41$ share a common factor greater than $1$ is $n = \boxed{23}$.
The answer is: 23

We know that $\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$.
Expanding $(\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w}) \times (\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w})$, we have
\begin{align*}
(\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w}) \times (\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w}) &= \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w} + \mathbf{w} \times \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w} \times \mathbf{w} \\
&= \mathbf{0} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w} + (-\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w}) + \mathbf{0} \\
&= \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w} + (-\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w}) \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 2 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}0\0\0\end{pmatrix}

